      "math_academy_id": "q-1952",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Given that $y={2}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={2}^{u},\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{u} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
