      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
